Rem ==================================================================
Rem iris-train.shoddy — TRAIN the species classifier. Fits a 4-8-3
Rem tanh/softmax network from neural.shoddy to the 120-row training
Rem split of Fisher's iris data by mini-batch SGD with momentum,
Rem evaluates it against the 30 held-out rows, and serializes the
Rem trained model — weights, head, AND input scaler — to
Rem dat/iris-model.bin, which iris.shoddy then loads.
Rem
Rem Build and run: build.cmd train        (Windows)
Rem                ./build.sh train        (Unix)
Rem Just run:      dotnet bin\iris-train.dll
Rem
Rem Run from THIS directory — the data paths dat/iris-train.dat and
Rem dat/iris-test.dat are relative, and the model lands in
Rem dat/iris-model.bin. Training takes a few seconds.
Rem
Rem This is the classification counterpart to the demographics mill,
Rem and the contrast is the point. Demographics predicts a NUMBER
Rem (income) and is scored on how close it gets. This predicts a
Rem CATEGORY (species) and is scored on how often it is right — and it
Rem reports how sure it was, which a regression network has no way to
Rem express.
Rem
Rem Three things it demonstrates that the older machine could not:
Rem   * Softmax outputs and cross-entropy loss, through the same
Rem     backward pass regression uses — see NetGradAt in neural.shoddy.
Rem   * Momentum (Moment = 0.9 in the Plan below), which is why 300
Rem     epochs suffice here where plain descent would want thousands.
Rem   * A Scaler stored INSIDE the model file. The measurements are raw
Rem     centimetres; the network needs them centred and scaled; and the
Rem     predictor must apply exactly the same transformation or it is
Rem     feeding the model inputs it was never fitted on. Writing the
Rem     scaler into the file is what makes that impossible to get wrong,
Rem     and the assert at the end of Main proves the two paths agree.
Rem
Rem Iris is the canonical classification data set — Fisher, 1936 —
Rem chosen here precisely because the expected result is known: setosa
Rem is linearly separable from the other two, and versicolor and
Rem virginica overlap along a handful of specimens. A working net gets
Rem the low hundreds of a percent either side of what this one gets, and
Rem anything wildly different means a bug rather than a discovery.
Rem
Rem What it gets, seeded and measured, in about four seconds:
Rem   train accuracy 0.9833 (118/120), test accuracy 0.90 (27/30)
Rem and all three test errors are virginica called versicolor. That gap
Rem between train and test is not a defect to tune away — it is the
Rem overlap, and the confusion matrix printed at the end shows it
Rem sitting exactly where the botany says it should. The three missed
Rem flowers are virginica specimens whose petals fall inside
Rem versicolor's range; the nearest neighbour of one of them, in the
Rem full data set, is a versicolor.
Rem
Rem Two honest caveats worth reading off that number:
Rem   * Thirty test rows means one row is worth 3.3 points, so 0.90 and
Rem     0.9333 are one flower apart. Across eight seeds this setup gives
Rem     27 or 28 correct — stable, but do not read precision into the
Rem     second digit.
Rem   * Two of the three errors come with the two candidate species
Rem     split near 0.53, which is the model saying it is guessing. The
Rem     third it announces at 0.999. A confident classifier is not
Rem     thereby a correct one, and this data set is small enough to show
Rem     you that in a single run.
Rem ==================================================================

Include "matrix.shoddy"
Include "file.shoddy"
Include "seq.shoddy"
Include "str.shoddy"
Include "neural.shoddy"
Include "math.shoddy"

Rem ---- the loader (mill-local: column-picking numeric CSV) -----------

Def DataLines(path As String) As List Of String
    Filter(ReadLines(path), Fn(s) => Len(s) > 0 And Not StartsWith(s, "#"))

Def ParseRow(line As String) As Array Of Number
    ToArray(Map(Split(line, ","), Fn(t) => Val(Trim(t))))

Rem Five columns: sepal length, sepal width, petal length, petal width,
Rem species. X is columns 1-4 in order; the species is column 5.
Def RowX(cells As Array Of Number) As Array Of Number
    ToArray({ Nth(cells, 1), Nth(cells, 2), Nth(cells, 3), Nth(cells, 4) })

Def RowY(cells As Array Of Number) As Number
    Nth(cells, 5)

Let species = 3

Def SpeciesName(k As Number) As String
    Select Case k
        Case 1
            "SETOSA"
        Case 2
            "VERSICOLOR"
        Case Else
            "VIRGINICA"

Rem ---- reporting -----------------------------------------------------

Def F4(x As Number) As String
    Str(RoundTo(x, 4))

Rem ---- the confusion matrix -------------------------------------------

Rem Accuracy alone hides WHICH way a classifier fails, and for this data
Rem the direction is the whole story. True species runs down the side,
Rem predicted across the top, so the diagonal is the correct answers and
Rem every off-diagonal cell names one specific confusion.
Rem Kron is the equality indicator, so the product of two Krons counts a
Rem row only when it is BOTH truly t and predicted p.
Def Tallied(net As Net, xs As List Of Array Of Number, ys As List Of Array Of Number, t As Number, p As Number) As Number
    Fold(ZipWith(xs, ys, Fn(x, y) => Kron(Argmax(y), t) * Kron(NetClassOf(net, x), p)), 0, +)

Def ConfRow(net As Net, xs As List Of Array Of Number, ys As List Of Array Of Number, t As Number)
    Print(PadLeft(SpeciesName(t), 12)
          & Fold(Map(Range(1, species), Fn(p) => PadLeft(Str(Tallied(net, xs, ys, t, p)), 12)), "", &))

Def Confusion(net As Net, xs As List Of Array Of Number, ys As List Of Array Of Number)
    Print(PadLeft("TRUE \\ PRED", 12)
          & Fold(Map(Range(1, species), Fn(p) => PadLeft(SpeciesName(p), 12)), "", &))
    Each(Range(1, species), Fn(t) => ConfRow(net, xs, ys, t))

Rem Called after every epoch; prints (and pays for the metrics) only
Rem every freq-th one.
Def Progress(epoch As Number, net As Net, xs As List Of Array Of Number, ys As List Of Array Of Number, freq As Number)
    If epoch Mod freq = 0 Then
        Print("EPOCH: " & Str(epoch) & "  LOSS = " & F4(NetLogLoss(net, xs, ys)) & "  ACC = " & F4(NetClassAccuracy(net, xs, ys)))

Def Main()
    Print("")
    Print("NEURAL NETWORK CLASSIFICATION - SHODDY - TRAINER")
    Print("FIT IRIS SPECIES TO SEPAL AND PETAL MEASUREMENTS")

    Print("")
    Print("LOADING TRAIN AND TEST DATA FROM FILE")
    Let trainRows = Map(DataLines("dat/iris-train.dat"), ParseRow)
    Let rawTrainX = Map(trainRows, RowX)
    Let trainY = Map(trainRows, Fn(r) => NetOneHot(RowY(r), species))
    Let testRows = Map(DataLines("dat/iris-test.dat"), ParseRow)
    Let rawTestX = Map(testRows, RowX)
    Let testY = Map(testRows, Fn(r) => NetOneHot(RowY(r), species))
    Print("LOADED " & Str(Length(trainRows)) & " TRAINING AND " & Str(Length(testRows)) & " TEST ROWS")

    Print("")
    Print("FIRST THREE X DATA (RAW CENTIMETRES):")
    Each(Taken(rawTrainX, 3), Print)
    Print("")
    Print("FIRST THREE TARGET Y (ONE-HOT SPECIES):")
    Each(Taken(trainY, 3), Print)

    Rem The scaler is fitted on the TRAINING columns only. Letting the
    Rem held-out rows influence the centring would leak them into
    Rem training and quietly flatter the test score.
    Print("")
    Print("FITTING INPUT SCALER TO THE TRAINING COLUMNS")
    Let scaler = ScalerFit(rawTrainX)
    Print("CENTRE:")
    Print(Center(scaler))
    Print("SPREAD:")
    Print(Spread(scaler))
    Let trainX = Map(rawTrainX, Fn(x) => ScalerApply(scaler, x))
    Let testX = Map(rawTestX, Fn(x) => ScalerApply(scaler, x))

    Rem one Seed call, so runs reproduce exactly
    Seed(0)

    Print("")
    Print("CREATING 4-8-3 TANH SOFTMAX NEURAL NETWORK")
    Let net0 = NetNewScaled(4, 8, species)
    Print("DONE")

    Let maxEpochs = 300
    Let plan = ClassPlan(0.1, 0.9, 10, maxEpochs)
    Let freq = maxEpochs / 10
    Print("")
    Print("PREPARING TRAIN PARAMETERS")
    Print("MAXEPOCHS = " & Str(Epochs(plan)))
    Print("LRNRATE = " & Str(Rate(plan)))
    Print("MOMENTUM = " & Str(Moment(plan)))
    Print("BATSIZE = " & Str(Batch(plan)))

    Print("")
    Print("STARTING (BATCH) TRAINING")
    Let net = NetFit(net0, plan, trainX, trainY, Fn(epoch, nn) => Progress(epoch, nn, trainX, trainY, freq))
    Print("DONE")

    Print("")
    Print("EVALUATING MODEL")
    Print("ACCURACY ON TRAIN DATA = " & F4(NetClassAccuracy(net, trainX, trainY)))
    Print("ACCURACY ON TEST DATA  = " & F4(NetClassAccuracy(net, testX, testY)))
    Print("CROSS-ENTROPY ON TEST  = " & F4(NetLogLoss(net, testX, testY)))

    Print("")
    Print("CONFUSION MATRIX ON TEST DATA")
    Confusion(net, testX, testY)

    Print("")
    Print("SERIALIZING MODEL TO dat/iris-model.bin")
    Let model = Model(net, Classify(), scaler)
    ModelSave("dat/iris-model.bin", model)

    Rem Reload and prove two separate things. First that the round trip
    Rem cost nothing: binary doubles are bit-exact, so the reloaded
    Rem model must predict EQUALLY, not merely closely. Second — and
    Rem this is the one that matters in practice — that feeding the
    Rem reloaded model a RAW row gives the same answer as feeding the
    Rem trained net a row we scaled by hand. That is the training and
    Rem serving paths agreeing, checked rather than assumed.
    Let check = ModelLoad("dat/iris-model.bin")
    Let raw1 = First(rawTestX)
    Assert(ModelPredict(check, raw1) = NetPredict(net, Classify(), First(testX)), "SERIALIZED MODEL PREDICTS BIT-EXACTLY FROM RAW INPUT")
    Print("DONE - ROUND TRIP IS BIT-EXACT AND SCALES RAW INPUT IDENTICALLY")

    Print("")
    Print("END TRAINING - RUN build.cmd run (OR ./build.sh run) TO CLASSIFY INTERACTIVELY")
